TELOMERE-CCC2-1:{"chunk":"c06","format":"ccc2-wire/1","records":[{"checksum":"9e962044394d0221c92c44f5a1b65002c997cb0f3b981fc714441959e53e2ef3","id":"C1","kind":"claim","label":"Anatomy of an independent Weil-positivity source (template)","note":"(i) Geometric space with torus: the Jacobian J(C), a complex torus. (ii) Correspondence/operator: Frobenius, eigenvalues π_i. (iii) Counting-born PSD form: deg ≥ 0 → discriminant condition. (iv) Zeros as eigenvalues; RH as the discriminant inequality. (v) Finite exact explicit formula (no truncation).","status":"exact","type":"C"},{"checksum":"3a4a9561b40309f4d380435674f32384eb8d1f0936c48cc27dededcee437e239","id":"C2","kind":"claim","label":"Zero-density audit (role, not a new estimate)","note":"Our detector bound N₊([T0−w,T0+w]) ≤ e^{1/2}W(h) used RH to interpret windows as critical-line zero counts. Unconditionally, off-line zeros pollute windows; zero-density estimates N(σ,T) bound how many can stray — they are the tool that would make the detector program unconditional. Classical estimates cited, not re-derived; the plug-in point is precise.","status":"heuristic","type":"C"},{"checksum":"457875d535c5ebb3558510b38446053eb6d8ca9391e2a8540e016b851261fa7a","id":"C3","kind":"claim","label":"K-theoretic home of the face-weights","note":"In H*_{S¹}(pt; R) = R[u], let E_z = (+)L_p over p≤z with equivariant Chern roots −u·log p (real coefficients legitimate). Then ch(λ_{−1}(E_z)) = ∏_p (1 − p^{−u}) = F_z(u), the equivariant K-theoretic Euler class. The face-weights are F_z′(1): the u-derivative at u=1 of the equivariant Euler class — differentiated residues. Only the primes are input (as Chern roots); the subset combinatorics, the signs, and the log magnitudes are all computed.","status":"exact","type":"C"},{"checksum":"269193cd6bf9ed1afc9846ab7a8dca7391d68a4281cfa0d40243ee2ba3398b1a","id":"C4","kind":"claim","label":"Refined positive region (stringy deformation)","note":"Positive region: the simplex Δ^{m−1} (m = π(z)), the face complex's geometric home, with its ordinary canonical form Ω (residues ±1). Stringy deformation: I(u) = ∫_Δ (∏_p x_p^{−u log p})Ω (Dirichlet integral; exponents are the prime logs). Differentiating at u=1 produces exactly Σ_S w(S) as generalized residues. This is Figueiredo's deformations move (D6) as the mechanism: her deformations are what produce non-±1 generalized residues; here the deformation parameter is u and the exponents are the prime logs. The bare simplex is contractible (D4); the arithmetic lives in the deformation, i.e., in the weights — exactly D4's conclusion, now with the obstruction explained and the refined object named.","status":"exact","type":"C"},{"checksum":"ee3791f0b6e3631e0cc2d6f871ae057c75cf52810840aebc195bcc573bc0dbe4","generates":[{"prescription":"count points: E: y²=x³+1/F₅ gives #E(F₅)=6, #E(F₂₅)=36, #E(F₁₂₅)=126; E/F₇: #E(F₇)=12, #E(F₄₉)=48 — each equals p^n+1−π^n−π̄^n exactly, no window","stage":"finite point count"},{"prescription":"form the Frobenius eigenvalues π,π̄ = (a_p ± √(a_p²−4p))/2 and the degree form Q(m,n) = deg(mφ+n) = p m² + a_p m n + n²; PSD because deg ≥ 0 (degree counts kernel points)","stage":"counting-born PSD form"},{"prescription":"read the discriminant: PSD ⟺ a_p²−4p ≤ 0 ⟺ |a_p| ≤ 2√p ⟺ |π| = √p — Hasse = RH for the curve","stage":"RH as discriminant"}],"honest_boundary":"The mechanism is cited classical theorem (Hasse/Weil), not re-derived. What we derived: the template anatomy (i)–(v) and the number-field ledger (HAVE/MISSING). Contrast with 10a-step3: the number-field explicit formula is infinite and needs windowing (Pareto frontier n ~ e^{7.4/w}); the function-field formula needs no window — the prime side is a finite point count.","honest_label":"computationally_verified","id":"D1","note":"by exhibited counting anchors: deg(1−φ) = #E(F_p) = 6, 12 (counted); deg(1+φ) = #twist(F_p) = 6, 4 (counted) = p+1+a_p. The positivity source is counting — the same kind as Λ ≥ 0 (D7/D9).","rule":"#E(F_{p^n}) = p^n + 1 − π^n − π̄^n (finite and exact);  Q(m,n) = deg(mφ+n) = p m² + a_p m n + n², PSD ⟹ a_p²−4p ≤ 0 ⟹ |π| = √p.","subtitle":"D10d — function-field / intersection-pairing template","type":"D","verified":[{"check":"point counts, |π|=√p to 6 digits","expected":"F₅: 6, 36, 126; F₇: 12, 48; zeta numerator roots |t| = 1/√p verified (RH for the curve)"},{"check":"discriminants and grid","expected":"−20 (F₅), −12 (F₇); Q ≥ 0 on grid"},{"check":"counting anchors","expected":"deg(1−φ) = #E(F_p) = 6, 12 (counted); deg(1+φ) = #twist(F_p) = 6, 4 (counted) = p+1+a_p"}]},{"checksum":"0ff93cf54ec3136f1307060274053e282a2246e6f6a74f27d0cf8bcc65a4cab8","generates":[{"prescription":"build P(t) = Σ_{n≤N0} a_n n^{it}, a_n = Λ(n)/√n, N0 = 50000 (5217 prime powers, S2 = 56.99); measure (1/2H)∫_{T0−H}^{T0+H} |P|²/S2 at T0 = 20000 for H = 200, 1000, 5000, 20000","stage":"mean value"},{"prescription":"check Σ_{r≤R}|P(t0+rδ)|² ≤ (N0+δ^{−1})S2 for δ = 1, 0.5","stage":"large sieve"},{"prescription":"tabulate prime data needed n = e^{7.433/w} against resolution at T0 = 100 (mean zero spacing 2.27)","stage":"uncertainty pricing"}],"honest_boundary":"10e's tools are analytic amplifiers, not independent positivity sources: they strengthen what the bridge says (mean-square control, unconditional leakage bounds) but add no new positivity and change nothing in the 10d ledger. Zero-density estimates are cited, not re-derived; the plug-in point is precise.","honest_label":"computationally_verified","id":"D2","note":"The mean-square amplifier is real on the detector's prime-side polynomial — it converts pointwise window counts into L² bounds. The uncertainty wall is the fundamental wall of the prime-side detector, sharp contrast with the function-field finite exact formula (10d), which needs no window at all.","rule":"P(t) = Σ_{n≤N0} a_n n^{it},  a_n = Λ(n)/√n, N0 = 50000;  Montgomery–Vaughan: (1/2H)∫_{T0−H}^{T0+H}|P|²/S2 → 1;  large sieve: Σ_{r≤R}|P(t0+rδ)|² ≤ (N0+δ^{−1})S2;  data cost: window width w needs n = e^{7.433/w}.","subtitle":"D10e — supporting tools: mean-value, large sieve, uncertainty","type":"D","verified":[{"check":"Montgomery–Vaughan at T0 = 20000","expected":"H=200: 0.9948; H=1000: 0.9789; H=5000: 0.9596; H=20000: 1.0073 — converges to 1"},{"check":"large sieve","expected":"δ=1: ratio 0.0041; δ=0.5: ratio 0.0080 — holds with headroom (bound crude for this polynomial, as expected)"},{"check":"uncertainty quantified","expected":"w=2 → 41; w=1 → 1.7e3; w=0.5 → 2.9e6; w=0.25 → 8.2e12; w=0.1 → 1.9e32. At T0=100 (spacing 2.27): w=1 resolves 0.44 spacings, w=0.5 resolves 0.22; isolating single zeros cleanly (w=0.25) needs 8e12 primes: infeasible"}]},{"checksum":"b8b1de9a71595fdc8901f47a624198c5b2df8f7beec1991a8947c58417cc0aea","generates":[{"prescription":"suppose an ordinary positive geometry (AHBL sense: canonical form with logarithmic poles, residues ±1 on boundary strata, ±1 at 0-dimensional strata); compare against |w({2})| = (log 2)/2 = 0.3466 ∉ {0,1}","stage":"obstruction"},{"prescription":"Proof 1 (subset expansion): F_z(u) = Σ_S (−1)^{|S|}(prod S)^{−u}; d/du gives Σ_S (−1)^{|S|+1}log(prod S)(prod S)^{−u} = Σ_S w(S) at u=1; regroup by containing prime: Σ_S w(S) = Σ_j (log p_j/p_j)∏_{i≠j}(1−1/p_i) = δ(z)Σ_j (log p_j)/(p_j−1)","stage":"identity, proof 1"},{"prescription":"Proof 2 (calculus): F_z′/F_z = Σ_p (p^{−u} log p)/(1−p^{−u}); at u=1: F_z′(1) = F_z(1)Σ_p (log p)/(p−1) = δ(z)Σ_p (log p)/(p−1)","stage":"identity, proof 2"},{"prescription":"verify numerically to 10 digits at z = 2..29; check asymptotic: δ(z) ~ e^{−γ}/log z, Σ_{p≤z}(log p)/(p−1) ~ log z (Mertens), so W(z) → e^{−γ} = 0.56146","stage":"numerical check"}],"honest_boundary":"Loophole named: weighted positive geometries allow general residues; the construction below (C4) is the natural such weighting — so the obstruction is really 'the ordinary notion is insufficient; the deformed notion is the home.' Distinct from D4's Σ_{prod S ≤ X} w(S) → 1: different regularization — all subsets of P_z here vs product-bounded there.","honest_label":"exact","id":"D3","note":"by two proofs of one identity: subset expansion (combinatorial) and log-derivative (calculus) agree. The identity computes the 2^{π(z)} subset sum in O(π(z)) operations. The literal question has a negative answer, by theorem — D4's 'no positive region found' is explained, not merely observed.","rule":"w(S) = (−1)^{|S|+1} log(prod S)/(prod S);  F_z(u) = ∏_{p≤z}(1 − p^{−u});  d/du F_z(u)|_{u=1} = Σ_S w(S) = δ(z)·Σ_{p≤z}(log p)/(p−1),  δ(z) = ∏_{p≤z}(1−1/p).","subtitle":"Node 14 — face-weights: obstruction, identity, differentiated residues","type":"D","verified":[{"check":"identity numerically","expected":"10 digits at z = 2..29 (face_weights.py)"},{"check":"asymptotic","expected":"W(z) → e^{−γ} = 0.56146; numerics: 0.4780 (z=29), 0.5153 (z=10³), 0.5333 (z=10⁵), climbing to 0.5615"},{"check":"obstruction values","expected":"|w({2})| = (log 2)/2 = 0.3466 not in {0,1}; signs (−1)^{|S|+1} compatible with residue alternation, magnitudes log(prod S)/(prod S) impossible"}]},{"checksum":"c4b758067f364f80a53a57fdae2daf4a05f9f53ca9d633e104b8e2e1ffeb73c7","from":"C1","id":"L1","rel":"defines","to":"D1","type":"L"},{"checksum":"cf9e2b8838083208463ed51ed7f79d7f0dc24e57754a64a09fcc065bea382e62","from":"D1","id":"L2","rel":"supports","to":"V1","type":"L"},{"checksum":"52f4e3c6493604e4393ca48048276e4b3f2f9b00dbdc803e790c8dcda2eadd42","from":"V1","id":"L3","rel":"refines","to":"V2","type":"L"},{"checksum":"8ba3e6cac4cd23f7038ef74ce7367700646c816daf397e112093bf98abdfa058","from":"D2","id":"L4","rel":"supports","to":"V3","type":"L"},{"checksum":"d3e5be638195be08acb6f81299556aa6fcd0bb615f35451b7903bb610b5c73e7","from":"C2","id":"L5","rel":"refines","to":"V3","type":"L"},{"checksum":"8e8fe3757dbb9abfd90769a932ebbc3d02c3526697640121b93853903eee75a0","from":"C3","id":"L6","rel":"defines","to":"D3","type":"L"},{"checksum":"ddec6ccb2942868c5d98c849b3eb51fea60512bdeb78ad15a8c382ec41432bb9","from":"C4","id":"L7","rel":"implements","to":"D3","type":"L"},{"checksum":"3f9f1248727da47e8f8fcdbc40b471f4ba08b3e20d566290a487a88fb6fd3572","consequence":"10a–10c assembled positivity inputs (Weil-type smoothed, Selberg-type optimized, primitive counting); 10d shows what would have to use them: a space + operator + counting-PSD form with zeros as eigenvalues. The missing object is operator-theoretic (a 'Frobenius for Δ'), not another positivity inequality. Reframes the hunt and closes the systematic pass: 10e audits the supporting analytic tools.","id":"V1","status":"exact","subject":"D10d template vs number-field program","type":"V"},{"checksum":"683b55f745a1206fbce1972a6840a05f247695d988101dd4e0c5d300d56ecfc5","consequence":"The 10d ledger: HAVE counting positivity (Λ ≥ 0, D7/D9 — same kind as deg ≥ 0), finite PD quadratic forms on faces (10c), an explicit formula (10a — but infinite, windowed). MISSING: a space playing the Jacobian's role (Δ_∞ is contractible, D4 — topologically trivial, so the object must live in weights/operators, not the bare space); a Frobenius-like operator with the zeros as eigenvalues; an intersection pairing tying zeros to the counting positivity. The torus in the RH story is the Jacobian — this answers the toroidal question: plane for zeta's domain, torus for the geometric explainer.","id":"V2","status":"exact","subject":"10d ledger","type":"V"},{"checksum":"5337412ab3bc6602682a85f5914eb4e0b0948860ef1483547d6609edc00834a4","consequence":"10e's tools are analytic amplifiers, not independent positivity sources. The systematic bridge pass 10a–10e is complete.","id":"V3","status":"exact","subject":"10e supporting tools","type":"V"}],"registry":{"C1":"C","C2":"C","C3":"C","C4":"C","D1":"D","D2":"D","D3":"D","L1":"L","L2":"L","L3":"L","L4":"L","L5":"L","L6":"L","L7":"L","V1":"V","V2":"V","V3":"V"},"wire_checksum":"e01a8602f9dc7ce717126aff15532c3018a52d21ad804518b083bad526477045"}:TELOMERE-CCC2-1